-- allium: 3
-- onboarding-guide.allium
--
-- Scope: The onboarding guide — one self-contained web page that lets anyone
--        curious try the board without installing anything. It is a playable
--        imitation of the board on invented example data, in two steps (the
--        board, then a graph of the same data), plus a plain-text walkthrough
--        of the same tour.
-- Includes: The page and where it lives (docs/guide/index.html, served by
--           GitHub Pages); its two steps and the Next/Back buttons between
--           them; what the example board holds; which keys the page answers
--           to and the rule that every one of them is a real binding
--           (key parity), with the test that holds the page to it; the
--           status-bar banner; the graph step; the walkthrough; the README
--           link to the page.
-- Excludes: What each key DOES on the real board (keybindings.allium and the
--           spec owning each action; the guide imitates those effects, it
--           does not redefine them). The real board's layout, columns and
--           sub-status sections (board-layout.allium), epics (epics.allium)
--           and work packages (work-packages.allium) — the guide shows
--           invented instances of them, nothing more. Colours, layout and
--           visual styling of the page: deliberately not specified.
-- Dependencies: KeyBinding, KeyNamespace from keybindings.allium.
--
-- Audience: anyone curious — a new teammate, a friend, a GitHub visitor. Most
-- only look briefly, so the guide has to work the moment the page opens.
--
-- Decisions taken with the user (task #86018), recorded so they are not
-- re-litigated:
--   - One self-contained HTML page, docs/guide/index.html, served by GitHub
--     Pages. No build step, no external scripts, no network fetches; opening
--     the file straight from disk works the same.
--   - It is an imitation on invented example data. It never talks to a real
--     store, MCP server or tmux.
--   - Two steps, ExploreBoard then Graph, moved between with on-page Next and
--     Back buttons. Buttons, not keys, so the guide invents no key of its own.
--   - Key parity is the most important rule: every key the guide answers to
--     is a real binding from the keybinding table. The guide may answer to a
--     subset of the table, never to a key or action the table lacks. The
--     direction is guide within table, not the reverse.
--   - The page declares the bindings it answers to in one embedded JSON
--     block; its key handling and its banner both read that block, and a test
--     reads it too.
--   - The page carries a plain-text walkthrough of the same tour, readable
--     without JavaScript, so an agent reading the file or a visitor who
--     prefers reading gets the whole story.
--   - The README links to the guide.

use "./keybindings.allium" as keybindings

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Whoever opens the page. No account, no identity: the page is public.
external entity Visitor {}

-- The repo's Rust test suite, the party the parity gate is demanded of.
external entity TestSuite {}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The parity gate: an obligation on the test suite, stated as a contract so
-- that removing the check is a visible spec change rather than a quiet test
-- deletion.
contract GuideKeyParityGate {
    -- For one entry of the page's dispatch-keys block: whether every key it
    -- declares is listed by a real row with the entry's namespace and action.
    entry_is_real_binding: (entry: GuideBinding) -> Boolean

    @invariant GuideBlockIsCheckedAgainstTheRealTable
        -- The Rust test
        -- src/keybindings/tests.rs::guide_declares_only_real_bindings reads
        -- docs/guide/index.html from the repo, parses the
        -- <script type="application/json" id="dispatch-keys"> block, and
        -- asserts, for every entry and every key in it, that some
        -- KEY_BINDINGS row has that namespace (by its published dotted name)
        -- and that action, and lists that key. This is
        -- GuideKeysAreRealBindings checked over the real page and the real
        -- table, not over a fixture.

    @invariant GuideBlockIsPresentAndNonEmpty
        -- Non-vacuity: the test fails if the page has no dispatch-keys
        -- block, if the block does not parse, or if it declares no entry.
        -- A missing block would make the parity check pass trivially while
        -- the page answers to keys nothing checks.

    @invariant OnlyThisDirectionIsChecked
        -- The test does not require the guide to declare every row. A row
        -- the guide leaves out is fine; a guide entry the table lacks fails.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The guide's two steps, in tour order.
enum GuideStep { explore_board | graph }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One entry of the page's dispatch-keys block:
--   <script type="application/json" id="dispatch-keys">
-- The block is a JSON array of objects {namespace, action, keys}:
--   namespace  the published dotted name of a KeyNamespace (board.normal,
--              board.detail, board.help, tmux.global, …)
--   action     the keybinding table's action id (navigate_row, exit_epic,
--              toggle_help, jump_to_dispatch, …)
--   keys       the keys, written exactly as the table writes them ("j",
--              "Space", "Esc", "Prefix+Space")
-- The block is the guide's one list of keys. The page's key handling and its
-- banner both read it; there is no second list in the page's script.
entity GuideBinding {
    namespace: keybindings/KeyNamespace
    action:    String
    keys:      Set<String>

    invariant DeclaresAtLeastOneKey {
        keys.count >= 1
    }
}

-- One visit to the page, from opening it to closing it. Nothing about a visit
-- is stored anywhere; it lives in the open page only.
entity GuideVisit {
    visitor: Visitor
    step:    GuideStep

    transitions step {
        explore_board -> graph
        graph -> explore_board
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == The two steps ==

rule GuideOpensOnTheBoard {
    when: GuideOpened(visitor)

    ensures: GuideVisit.created(visitor: visitor, step: explore_board)

    @guidance
        -- Opening the page from GitHub Pages or straight from disk is the
        -- same event; the page fetches nothing either way.
}

rule NextGoesToTheGraph {
    when: GuideNextPressed(visit)

    requires: visit.step = explore_board

    ensures: visit.step = graph

    @guidance
        -- An on-page button, not a key. The graph shows the same example
        -- data the board step showed.
}

rule BackGoesToTheBoard {
    when: GuideBackPressed(visit)

    requires: visit.step = graph

    ensures: visit.step = explore_board

    @guidance
        -- An on-page button, not a key. The board comes back where the
        -- visitor left it: cursor, open epic or package, open overlay.
}

-- == Keys ==

rule GuideKeyRunsItsDeclaredAction {
    -- The guide answers a key only through the dispatch-keys block: it finds
    -- the entry in the namespace the imitation is in that lists the key, and
    -- imitates that entry's action as the real board would.
    when: GuideKeyPressed(visit, namespace, key)

    requires: visit.step = explore_board

    let binding = GuideBindings.any(g => g.namespace = namespace and key in g.keys)

    requires: binding != null

    ensures: GuideActionImitated(visit: visit, namespace: namespace, action: binding.action, key: key)

    @guidance
        -- The namespace is the mode the imitation is in, decided as on the
        -- real board: board.normal on the board, board.detail with the
        -- detail panel open, board.help with the ? overlay open, tmux.global
        -- inside the fake agent window.
        -- The tour, as agreed: move around the board; enter epics and work
        -- packages with Space and leave them with q / Esc / Q; open the
        -- detail panel; open a fake agent window for a running task with
        -- Space and come back with Prefix+Space (tmux.global
        -- jump_to_dispatch); open the ? help overlay.
        -- Prefix is Ctrl+b, tmux's default; the agent window's banner says so.
        -- Each block entry also carries the guide's own imitation of the
        -- row's context, so q is exit_epic inside a view and quit (shown as
        -- a note, never acted on) on the top-level board, as on the real
        -- board. The parity test checks namespace, action, key and
        -- description, not that context.
}

rule GuideKeyOutsideTheBlockDoesNothing {
    -- The converse: a key the block does not list for the current namespace
    -- does nothing, as on the real board (keybindings.allium:
    -- KeyWithoutARowCannotAct). The Graph step declares no keys, so every key
    -- does nothing there.
    when: GuideKeyPressed(visit, namespace, key)

    requires: visit.step != explore_board
              or not GuideBindings.any(g => g.namespace = namespace and key in g.keys)

    ensures: GuideKeyIgnored(visit: visit, namespace: namespace, key: key)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Key parity, the most important rule of the guide. Every (namespace, action,
-- key) the guide declares is listed by some row of the real keybinding table
-- with that namespace and that action. The guide may declare a subset of the
-- table, never a key or action the table lacks. Checked by
-- GuideKeyParityGate.
invariant GuideKeysAreRealBindings {
    for g in GuideBindings:
        g.keys.all(k =>
            keybindings/KeyBindings.any(b =>
                b.namespace = g.namespace
                and b.action = g.action
                and k in b.keys))
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A visitor reading or playing the page. Anyone may.
actor GuideReader {
    identified_by: Visitor where true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface OnboardingGuidePage {
    facing reader: GuideReader
    context visit: GuideVisit where visitor = reader

    exposes:
        visit.step
        for binding in GuideBindings:
            binding.namespace
            binding.action
            binding.keys

    provides:
        GuideOpened(reader)
        GuideNextPressed(visit) when visit.step = explore_board
        GuideBackPressed(visit) when visit.step = graph
        GuideKeyPressed(visit, namespace, key)

    @guarantee OnePageNoBuildNoNetwork
        -- The guide is one HTML file, docs/guide/index.html, served by
        -- GitHub Pages. It has no build step, loads no external script and
        -- makes no network fetch; opened as a plain file from disk it works
        -- the same. Nothing has to be installed to use it.

    @guarantee ImitationOnlyNeverTheRealThing
        -- Everything the page shows is invented example data. The page never
        -- talks to a real store, MCP server or tmux, and nothing a visitor
        -- does on it reaches any of them.

    @guarantee ExampleBoardIsLarge
        -- The ExploreBoard step is the kanban board with its four columns
        -- (Backlog, Running, Review, Done) and a large example board: many
        -- epics, including epics nested inside epics; a big backlog;
        -- running tasks spread over several sub-status sections (for
        -- example active, needs input, stale, crashed); review tasks in
        -- several review sections; and at least two work packages whose
        -- cards sit on the main board.

    @guarantee BannerListsTheKeysThatApplyHere
        -- A banner, the imitation of the board's status bar, is always shown
        -- in the ExploreBoard step and lists the keys that apply where the
        -- cursor is. It is built from the dispatch-keys block, so it never
        -- names a key the page does not answer to, and a key the page
        -- answers to in that place is not left off it.

    @guarantee GraphShowsHowTheBoardOrganizesWork
        -- The Graph step draws the same example data as a node-link graph,
        -- to make the difference between the two groupings plain:
        --   - a tree from the board through each epic and sub-epic to every
        --     task, so every task visibly lives in one place;
        --   - the work packages as nodes beside it, each linking into the
        --     tree: a line to each task it links, and to a bracket over all
        --     of a linked epic's tasks.
        -- Choosing a package (an on-page button, or the package's node)
        -- lights up its links and the branches they land on, and dims the
        -- rest. The chosen package is also laid out as its own four-column
        -- board of the tasks and epic cards it links, each task saying
        -- which epic it comes from. #whole-picture links straight to this
        -- step, #whole-picture/<package id> with that package chosen. The
        -- step also gives a sense of how much the board holds. It is not
        -- operated with keys.

    @guarantee WalkthroughReadsWithoutJavaScript
        -- The page carries a plain-text walkthrough of the same tour in its
        -- HTML, readable with JavaScript off, so an agent reading the file
        -- or a visitor who prefers reading gets the whole story: the board
        -- and its columns; sub-statuses; epics and auto-dispatch; work
        -- packages; agents working in worktrees and tmux windows; skills,
        -- the knowledge base and the MCP tools at a high level; and a link
        -- to install via the README.

    @guarantee ReadmeLinksToTheGuide
        -- The repo's README links to the guide.

    @guidance
        -- Colours and layout are deliberately left to the page.
}

surface GuideKeyParityTestSuite {
    facing caller: TestSuite

    contracts:
        demands GuideKeyParityGate
}
